execute [SimTalk] - Method
Runs the Method designated by the reference operator <&> as a subroutine.
Remarks
The execution of the calling Method will be interrupted until the called Method is finished.
The method execute is especially handy if the path to a Method is stored in a Variable of data type object.
Type
Method
Syntax
<&>Method.execute([Argument1:any, ... ][, @:object=@, ?:object=self])
Parameters
The optional parameters Argument of data type any designate the arguments which are to be executed.
If you type in two additional optional parameters of data type object, then the parameter @ is assigned to
the anonymous identifier @ and the parameter ? is assigned self.
Example
Variable := &MyMethod1      // variable is of data type object
Variable.execute            // executes MyMethod1 
&MyMethod2.execute("abc")   // executes myMethod2 
                            // that expects 1 parameter
                            // of data type string
self.execute(n+1)           // calls a method recursively 
Note
You can call the method execute from Python. In this case execute will be executed like a formula.
Therefore you cannot move or delete MUs in the Method, and you cannot use a wait-instruction, a
waituntil-instruction, or a stopuntil-instruction.
In addition,Plant Simulation does not show the tab Expressions of the Method Debugger.
# call myMethod from Python 
res = current.myMethod.execute()
print(res)
execute [SimTalk] - Method
