executeIn [SimTalk]
Calls the Method or the user-defined attribute of data type method designated by the reference operator
<&> after the specified number of seconds of simulation time has elapsed.
Remarks
Plant Simulation enters a MethCall event into the list of scheduled events in the EventController at the
simulation time which is the current simulation time plus the time you passed as the parameter CallAt.
Since the Method will be called at some time in the future, the parameter CallAt must be greater than or
equal to 0. If events are already scheduled at this time, the MethCall will be appended at the end of the
existing simultaneous events.
In the called Method the anonymous identifier ? references the caller, i.e., the Method which contained the
Method executeIn.
If the caller is a user-defined attribute of data type method, it references the location of this user-defined
attribute. The anonymous identifier @ references the EventController.
Type
Method
Syntax
<&>Method.executeIn(CallAt:time[, Argument1:any, ...])
<&>Method.executeIn(CallAt:dateTime[, Argument1:any, ...])
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter CallAt of data type time designates the time in seconds after which the Method will be
called. Instead, you can also pass a value of data type dateTime. Then the Method will be called at the
specified point in time.
• The following optional parameters (Argument1, ...) are passed to the Method when it is being called.
Example
&MyMethod1.executeIn(23.5) 
// throttle the engine in 5 seconds to 85 percent
&ThrottleEngine.executeIn(5, 0.85)
// New year's day 2025 midnight, year-month-day notation
&MyMethod2.executeIn(str_to_date("2025/12/31")) 
// New year's day 2025 midnight, day-month-year notation
executeIn [SimTalk]
11-3710 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

&MyMethod2.executeIn(str_to_date("31.12.2025")) 
// call the method with a local variable reference, passing two parameters 
to the method
var obj: object := &MyMethod3
obj.executeIn(4, "my string", true)
